L["palm-sunday"] = """Jesus and his disciples were nearing Jerusalem. It was the week of Passover, and the city was packed with pilgrims. When they reached the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples ahead. Go to the village ahead of you, and at once you will find a donkey tied there, with her colt by her. Untie them and bring them to me. If anyone says anything to you, say that the Lord needs them, and he will send them right away.

This took place to fulfill what was spoken through the prophet Zechariah. Say to Daughter Zion, see, your king comes to you, gentle and riding on a donkey, and on a colt, the foal of a donkey. Kings of war rode horses. A king who comes in peace rode a donkey. Jesus was making a deliberate statement.

The disciples did as Jesus had instructed. They brought the donkey and the colt and placed their cloaks on them for Jesus to sit on. A very large crowd spread their cloaks on the road, while others cut branches from the trees and spread them on the road. Laying down cloaks was how people honored a king.

The crowds that went ahead of him and those that followed shouted, hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest heaven! Hosanna means save us. These words came from Psalm 118, a song about the coming of God's chosen one. When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred and asked, who is this? The crowds answered, this is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee.

Some of the Pharisees in the crowd said to Jesus, teacher, rebuke your disciples. He replied, I tell you, if they keep quiet, the stones will cry out. Luke adds that as he approached the city, he saw it and wept over it. If you, even you, had only known on this day what would bring you peace. But now it is hidden from your eyes.

Why does this matter? This is the only time in the Gospels that Jesus accepted public praise as the king. He had spent his ministry telling people to keep quiet about who he was. Now, as he moved toward the cross, he let the praise happen. But he came as a humble king, riding a donkey, not as the warrior many people wanted.

What happened after? Within days, the cheering crowd was gone. Jesus cleared the temple, taught in the courts, and was challenged by the leaders. By Friday, voices in a crowd were shouting a different word, crucify him. Many who welcomed a king on Sunday wanted a different kind of king than the one they got.

What does this mean for us today? We are tempted to want Jesus on our terms, as a king who solves our problems the way we want. Palm Sunday invites us to welcome him as he is, humble and gentle, and to let him be Lord of our lives. It also reminds us that he is willing to go to the cross for us. The donkey was taking him there.

That is why this story is so important. It says that the King has come, gentle and humble, bringing peace. And that every Palm Sunday since has been a day to say, hosanna, save us."""

L["tribute"] = """After Jesus entered Jerusalem, the religious leaders were looking for a way to trap him. The Pharisees went out and laid plans to trap him in his words. They sent their disciples to him along with the Herodians. These two groups usually disliked each other. The Pharisees resented Roman rule, and the Herodians supported Herod and the Romans. But they were willing to work together against Jesus.

Teacher, they said, we know that you are a man of integrity and that you teach the way of God in accordance with the truth. You aren't swayed by others, because you pay no attention to who they are. Tell us then, what is your opinion? Is it right to pay the imperial tax to Caesar or not? It began with flattery, and it hid a trap. If Jesus said yes, he would lose the support of the crowds who hated the tax. If he said no, he could be reported to Rome as a rebel.

But Jesus knew their evil intent. Why are you trying to trap me, you hypocrites? Show me the coin used for paying the tax. They brought him a denarius, and he asked them, whose image is this? And whose inscription? Caesar's, they replied. Then he said to them, give back to Caesar what is Caesar's, and to God what is God's.

When they heard this, they were amazed. So they left him and went away. His answer avoided the trap and said something deeper. The coin had Caesar's face on it, so it was fair to give it back to him. But people bear another image. Genesis says that God made human beings in his own image. If the coin belongs to Caesar because it carries his image, then people belong to God because they carry his.

Why does this matter? Jesus showed that he was not caught between politics and religion. He accepted that we have duties to earthly governments, and he put those duties in their place. The final claim on our lives belongs to God.

What happened after? The questions kept coming that day. The Sadducees asked about the resurrection, and a scribe asked which commandment was the greatest. Jesus answered them all. Then he asked them a question that no one could answer. After that, no one dared to ask him any more questions.

What does this mean for us today? Many people feel torn between loyalty to their country and loyalty to their faith. Jesus says that we can pay our taxes, obey the law and be good citizens, and that none of it replaces what we owe to God. It also asks us a pointed question. If the coin has Caesar's image, what bears God's image? We do. Everything we are belongs to him. And it is a reminder not to be taken in by flattery, which can be the first step of a trap.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God has the first claim on our lives, because we are made in his image. And it shows Jesus' wisdom in the face of people who wanted to catch him."""
